partialText:
&lt;p&gt;I kept starting learning roadmaps (DSA, system design, AI engineering) and then losing track: what I'd covered, how much time I'd really put in, and what was next. Spreadsheets didn't stick. So I built &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://gradiaqo.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gradiaqo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, a free app that turns any subject into a study plan and tracks your progress.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ready-made paths&lt;/strong&gt;: DSA, System Design, AI Engineer and Data Scientist&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your own paths&lt;/strong&gt;: build one in the app, or import it from a spreadsheet or YAML file&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Study sessions&lt;/strong&gt;: log your time and watch your progress add up&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Streaks and analytics&lt;/strong&gt; to keep you going, plus an "interview ready" view&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Organizations&lt;/strong&gt;, so a team or a coding club can share paths&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's a Turborepo monorepo with Yarn 4:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;API&lt;/strong&gt;: NestJS, TypeORM and Postgres. Request and response schemas use TypeBox, and CASL handles permissions (who can edit which path).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Web&lt;/strong&gt;: React 19 and Vite.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Shared packages&lt;/strong&gt;: the database entities, the schemase in their own packages, so the API, the web app and a
small CLI all use the same code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tests&lt;/strong&gt;: Vitest with a 100% coverage requirement, Planst a real database, and Stryker mutation testing to check
that the tests actually catch bugs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Hosting: one small node
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Production is a single-node k3s cluster on Civo (1 CPU, 2ncluding the storage volume.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traefik&lt;/strong&gt; takes the traffic and &lt;strong&gt;cert-manager&lt;/strong&gt; getses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Caddy&lt;/strong&gt; serves the built React app and proxies everything else to the API, so the app and the API share one origin. That keeps the
refresh-token cookie simple: no cross-site cookie headach&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub Actions&lt;/strong&gt; builds the images and pushes them to A deploy is a &lt;code&gt;kubectl rollout restart&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backups&lt;/strong&gt;: a nightly CronJob runs &lt;code&gt;pg_dump&lt;/code&gt;, encryptsnd uploads it to OneDrive. It keeps 30 days. I tested a
restore before trusting it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Things that surprised me
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. My cloud provider blocks outgoing email ports.&lt;/strong&gt; Civverification emails just timed out. Brevo, my SMTP&lt;br&gt;
provider, also accepts port 2525, which fixed it. Check t it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Postgres reorders your JSON.&lt;/strong&gt; I store some item detmporter compared the stored value with the file to decide&lt;br&gt;
whether anything changed, and every re-import "changed" eep key order. Comparing with sorted keys fixed it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Old Traefik on managed k3s.&lt;/strong&gt; The provider's Traefik custom resource definitions or an IngressClass. Middleware&lt;br&gt;
like the http → https redirect needed the older `traefik. Ingress is silently ignored, check your Traefik version&lt;br&gt;
first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;**4. A single-page app is invisible to search engines by al &lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt;, a &lt;code&gt;sitemap.xml&lt;/code&gt;, page titles, canonical&lt;br&gt;
links and Open Graph tags, Google showed "No information and link previews were blank. Build those into the buildstep: my Vite config now generates &lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt; and the sitemap for the production domain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. CI doesn't need to run for a README change.&lt;/strong&gt; A smalged and skips the test jobs when only docs or deploy files&lt;br&gt;
changed. Branch protection still passes, because the skip&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Public pages for each learning path, so people can brow&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pre-rendering the homepage for search engines&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Study reminders&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it and tell me what's broken
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's early, and I'd love feedback from anyone preparing fething new: what's confusing, what's missing, and what&lt;br&gt;
would make you open it every day?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://gradiaqo.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;gradiaqo.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm happy to go deeper on any part of the stack in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a workforce calendar, shifts, absences, and days off can look like variations of the same event. They aren't. A day off belongs to a calendar date; a shift belongs to a span of time. Mixing those rules can move an event to the wrong day without producing an obvious error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I worked on a workforce calendar using React, TypeScript, and Bryntum. It combined shifts, absences, overtime, and days off from separate services. They looked similar in the calendar, but their date handling and visibility rules were not interchangeable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A day off that moves to yesterday
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take a day-off record with the value &lt;code&gt;2026-10-23&lt;/code&gt;. Parsing that string as midnight UTC and displaying it in Los Angeles can produce October 22. The browser has converted an instant correctly; the mistake is that a day off was never meant to be an instant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Input&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Incorrect interpretation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Expected result&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;2026-10-23&lt;/code&gt; — date-only day off&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Oct 22&lt;/strong&gt; — UTC midnight formatted in Los Angeles&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Oct 23&lt;/strong&gt; — preserve the intended calendar day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A shift timestamp is different: it represents a moment that may be displayed at different local times. We kept those two paths separate. Here's a reduced TypeScript illustration of that boundary:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TypeScript · illustrative example (not production code)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CalendarRecord&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;dayOff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;startsAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;toCalendarEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CalendarRecord&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;dayOff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Keep the calendar date as a date, not a UTC timestamp.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;dayOff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;calendarDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A timestamp represents an instant; localize at display time.&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;startsAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;startsAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The important choice is that the date-only branch does not call &lt;code&gt;new Date()&lt;/code&gt; or silently apply timezone conversion. In a real application, the downstream rendering adapter also needs to preserve that meaning. I would test the same record across timezones and calendar views, not just test the conversion function in isolation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The boundary before Bryntum
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The services weren't returning one shared event format. We could have mapped each response directly into the calendar, then handled the differences in individual views. I chose a typed application event model instead. It gave IDs, categories, date handling, and permission metadata a consistent shape before anything reached Bryntum.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where service-specific data stops and calendar rendering begins&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;SERVICE CONTRACTS
  Shifts · overtime           Absences · days off
  Identifiers and time fields Categories and visibility rules
                 ↓
        Typed application event model
        Stable identity · date handling · categories
        Permission metadata
                 ↓
  Product rules               View adapters
  Normalized events           Day · week · month · team
  Consistent access rules
                 ↓
    Bryntum: event layout, interactions, and presentation
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The extra mapping layer meant more code and more tests. The alternative was to maintain the same conversion and visibility logic in several places, including day, week, month, and team views. I preferred one boundary where the behavior could be reviewed and tested.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A responsive calendar still needs fresh data
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Calendar navigation creates an awkward balance. People move between weeks and return to ranges they recently viewed. Fetching everything again makes the UI feel slow; never refreshing cached results can leave an old schedule on screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: moving across weeks, then returning&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Step&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Range&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Action&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;01&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Week 42&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Load the visible range&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;02&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Week 43&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Navigate forward&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;03&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Week 42&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reuse cached results; revalidate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We reused loaded ranges, debounced quick navigation, and refreshed in the background when needed. The selected date and view could be restored, but the schedule itself still needed revalidation. Another important check was ensuring a late response from an earlier range could not overwrite the one currently on screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I checked beyond the happy path
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A populated calendar isn't proof that its data is right. The cases worth checking were the quiet failures: date-only records shifting days, confidential absence details showing in another view, cached ranges not refreshing, and an old request returning after the user had navigated somewhere else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I would carry into the next scheduling project is not a particular calendar configuration. It's the decision to make dates, permissions, and data freshness explicit before rendering. That gives the UI library less to guess about, and gives the rest of the application rules that can be tested without opening a calendar.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://nehamangla.com/blog/reliable-scheduling-uis" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nehamangla.com&lt;/a&gt;.Explore more of my engineering work and articles on my portfolio.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shaders sums up its workflow in one README sentence: "Most people don't write their effects by hand. They design them." The library gives you 200+ WebGPU effects as components, and the README documents a workflow that starts in a visual editor at shaders.com, which exports a component tree ready to paste, while the CLI can write selected shaders into your project. The README also puts your coding agent in the middle of that loop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The shaders package is described as WebGPU effects as components for React, Vue, Svelte, Solid and plain JavaScript, installed with &lt;code&gt;npm install shaders&lt;/code&gt;. The engine, every component and the framework bindings are MIT licensed. The design editor, presets and other platform features at shaders.com are separate and have their own terms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Layers that blend on the GPU
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every effect is a component. A &lt;code&gt;Shader&lt;/code&gt; component renders the canvas, and its children are layers, evaluated top to bottom and blended on the GPU. The README's examples import &lt;code&gt;Shader&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;LinearGradient&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;CursorTrail&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;shaders/react&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;shaders/vue&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;shaders/svelte&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;shaders/solid&lt;/code&gt;, with the same props in every framework. Plain JavaScript gets &lt;code&gt;createShader&lt;/code&gt;, which takes a DOM element and a list of components with their props.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The effect categories in the README include gradients, noise, glass, metal, light, distortions, transitions, blurs and cursor effects, and you can nest, blend and mask them. It also lists TypeScript, typed props with reactive updates and SSR safety. The usage claim, "thousands of websites" and "16,000+ design engineers", is the project's own and I have no way to check it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  From editor to files
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The design editor is an infinite canvas where you stack components, tune props with real controls, and drive props from the cursor or a timeline. Export Code produces the component tree for your framework. The README says design and export are free with an account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CLI is what turns that into a maintained part of a codebase. &lt;code&gt;npx shaders connect&lt;/code&gt; links the codebase to a Shaders project, &lt;code&gt;install&lt;/code&gt; writes component files from it, &lt;code&gt;search&lt;/code&gt; finds a preset by description, &lt;code&gt;preview&lt;/code&gt; shows it running before you install, and &lt;code&gt;update&lt;/code&gt; pulls in what you changed in the editor. The README says it detects your framework, writes to your components folder, and records what it installed in a lock file, with nothing installed globally. I like that the CLI records what it installs in a lock file and pulls editor changes into the codebase with &lt;code&gt;update&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Handing it to an agent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The "Using Shaders with AI" section offers three routes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An agent skill, installed with &lt;code&gt;npx shaders skill&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;npx skills add shader-effects-inc/shaders&lt;/code&gt;, that teaches the agent the component library, how layers compose, and the CLI workflow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An MCP server configured with &lt;code&gt;npx shaders@latest install-mcp&lt;/code&gt; for Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Windsurf, Copilot and others. Per the README, the agent can find, install and edit the shaders you design, on any account including free ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;llms.txt&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;llms-full.txt&lt;/code&gt; files, the second carrying the full component reference with props, defaults and ranges.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last one is the one I would try first, because &lt;code&gt;llms-full.txt&lt;/code&gt; carries the component reference with props, defaults and ranges.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Writing your own effect
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When none of the 200+ components fits, the README shows &lt;code&gt;defineShader&lt;/code&gt;: a plain object with a name, its props and what to draw at each pixel. The example &lt;code&gt;Halo&lt;/code&gt; component takes a color, a center and a radius, and its &lt;code&gt;paint&lt;/code&gt; field is a short WGSL snippet. The guide covers two ways to write the pixel part. Composing it from std primitives is marked experimental. Writing WGSL directly is marked stable. I would take the stable path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Free and Pro
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The README separates free from paid. The README says everything it lists before its Pro section is free, including the editor, export, CLI and AI integrations. Pro adds over a thousand presets, website sections, watermark-free HD video and image rendering, a Framer plugin, unlimited version history in the editor, and Pro presets through the CLI and MCP. For my own projects, the question would be less about price and more about how much UI work I want tied to an editor account; the npm package itself is MIT licensed, while the editor and platform features have separate terms.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/shader-effects-inc/shaders" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/shader-effects-inc/shaders&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Curated by &lt;a href="https://www.agentpalisade.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Agent Palisade&lt;/a&gt; — practical AI for small and mid-sized businesses.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have a day job as a software engineer at a bank. In the evenings, I build my own things. One of them is ChordPic, a small tool for making guitar chord diagrams. People from all over the world started using it, and at some point I wanted it to speak their language.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I knew exactly what that would mean. At work, I've used the usual i18n libraries in more than one app, and I didn't want to bring any of that into a project I build in my free time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The part nobody puts in the README
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've done this, you know the drill. Every piece of text in your app turns into a key. &lt;code&gt;"Create chord chart"&lt;/code&gt; becomes &lt;code&gt;t('editor.toolbar.create')&lt;/code&gt;. The actual sentence moves to &lt;code&gt;en.json&lt;/code&gt;. Then you copy &lt;code&gt;en.json&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;de.json&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;fr.json&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;es.json&lt;/code&gt;, and so on, and you get them translated somehow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And then you keep shipping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You change a button label, and now eleven files are out of date. You add a feature and forget one key in one language, and someone in Brazil sees &lt;code&gt;editor.toolbar.share_tooltip&lt;/code&gt; on their screen. You open a component and can't read it anymore, because all the words are gone, replaced by dotted paths. You spend an evening on a "quick" copy change and most of it goes into JSON files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a big company with a localization team, maybe that's fine. For one person working evenings, it was the reason I kept putting off translating at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I actually wanted
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I wanted was simple: write my app in English, the way I always do, and have it show up in other languages. No keys. No files to keep in sync. If I change a sentence, every language should follow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So in December 2022 I started building it for myself. That became &lt;a href="https://magictranslate.io" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Magic Translate&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It works like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@magic-translate/react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Toolbar&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Create chord chart&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That's the whole idea. The text stays in your component. Magic Translate translates it with AI the first time it's seen, caches it, and serves the cached version after that. Change the sentence, and it gets translated again. Adding a new language is a single line of code, instead of translating hundreds of keys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Along the way it grew into something I'm honestly proud of:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It renders translations on the server for Next.js and React Server Components, so search engines see real translated pages, not English that gets swapped out later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;There are clients for React, Vue, Angular and plain JavaScript.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you don't like a translation, you can override it by hand and your override wins.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ChordPic now runs in 13 languages, including Arabic, Hindi, Persian and Chinese. I didn't write a single translation file for it. When I change some text on the site, I don't think about languages at all anymore. That still feels a bit like magic to me, which is where the name came from.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My favorite moments are when a ChordPic user writes to me and asks for their language. I add one line, and it's live in production the same day. Seeing someone's reply when the thing they asked for in the morning is there by the evening never gets old.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  And then I tried to tell people about it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part I didn't expect to be the hard one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Search for "react i18n" and count the libraries. Then the translation management platforms. Then the "AI translation for your app" startups that seem to launch every week. The space is incredibly crowded. Whenever I post about Magic Translate, it disappears into that sea within a day. To a stranger scrolling past, it looks like one more i18n tool, and I get why. I'd scroll past it too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But here's the thing I keep coming back to. If there are this many solutions, the problem isn't solved. Nobody builds the hundredth library for something that already works well. Every new i18n tool exists because somebody, somewhere, got as fed up with key files as I did. The sheer number of options isn't a sign that the space is done. It's a sign that developers are still looking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I believe the answer is to stop making developers manage translations at all. I might be wrong about that. But I've been using it in production for years now, and I'm not going back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  So, a question for you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm good at building things. I'm clearly not good at getting them seen. So I'd love to hear from you:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How do you deal with translations in your apps today? What annoys you most?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you've launched a developer tool into a crowded space, what actually worked for you?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And if you're curious, try it on a side project and tell me what's wrong with it: &lt;a href="https://magictranslate.io" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;magictranslate.io&lt;/a&gt;. There's a free plan, and I read every message I get.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thanks for reading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What Is Redux Toolkit?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redux Toolkit (RTK) is a library used to manage global state in React applications. It is the official, recommended way to write Redux logic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In React, we use &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; to manage data inside a component. But when multiple components need the same data, sharing and updating that data can become difficult.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, in an e-commerce application, the Navbar displays the cart count, and the Cart component displays the cart items. Both components need access to the same data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redux Toolkit solves this problem by storing shared data in one central place called the &lt;strong&gt;Redux Store&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fechdog0tm9ocprjiilez.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fechdog0tm9ocprjiilez.png" alt="diagram" width="800" height="567"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Important Concepts
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Store:&lt;/strong&gt; Stores the application's global state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Slice:&lt;/strong&gt; Contains a piece of state and the functions used to update it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reducer:&lt;/strong&gt; Defines how the state changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;useSelector:&lt;/strong&gt; Reads data from the Redux Store.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;useDispatch:&lt;/strong&gt; Dispatches actions to update the state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How to Use Redux Toolkit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, install Redux Toolkit and React Redux:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; @reduxjs/toolkit react-redux
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Create a Slice
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create a file named &lt;code&gt;counterSlice.js&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;createSlice&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@reduxjs/toolkit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;counterSlice&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createSlice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="na"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="na"&gt;reducers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;counterSlice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;actions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;counterSlice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, &lt;code&gt;createSlice()&lt;/code&gt; creates a slice with an initial state and a reducer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the &lt;code&gt;increment&lt;/code&gt; action is dispatched, the count increases by one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Create the Store
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create a file named &lt;code&gt;store.js&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;configureStore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@reduxjs/toolkit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;counterReducer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;./counterSlice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;configureStore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;counterReducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;configureStore()&lt;/code&gt; function creates the Redux Store and registers the counter reducer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Connect Redux to React
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;main.jsx&lt;/code&gt;, wrap the application with &lt;code&gt;Provider&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react-redux&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;./store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;./App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Provider&lt;/code&gt; makes the Redux Store accessible to the components inside it. Include this wrapper in your existing React rendering code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Use Redux in a Component
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;App.jsx&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useSelector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useDispatch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react-redux&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;./counterSlice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useSelector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useDispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Count: &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        Increment
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, &lt;code&gt;useSelector()&lt;/code&gt; reads the count from the store, and &lt;code&gt;useDispatch()&lt;/code&gt; allows us to dispatch an action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the button is clicked, the &lt;code&gt;increment&lt;/code&gt; action is dispatched, the reducer updates the count, and React displays the new value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redux Toolkit makes global state management easier in React by providing a central store and a structured way to update shared data. It is especially useful in applications where multiple components need to access and update the same state.&lt;br&gt;
For small applications, &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useContext&lt;/code&gt; may be enough. For larger applications with complex shared state, Redux Toolkit can make state management easier to organize and maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

